WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.475 --> 00:00:03.495
This is why I think "Anatomy of A Murder" is the best courtroom

00:00:03.495 --> 00:00:06.415
drama ever made. First, the case.

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:09.825
Before the movie begins, Lieutenant Frederick Manion

00:00:09.825 --> 00:00:12.795
shot and killed innkeeper Barney Quill, one hour

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.680
after Manion's wife, Laura, arrives home bruised

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.540
saying Quill raped and beat her in the woods.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.960
The District Attorney is going to prosecute Manion

00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:25.015
for first degree murder, so Manion hires Paul Biegler

00:00:25.015 --> 00:00:25.975
to defend him.

00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:27.985
Second: The complications

00:00:27.985 --> 00:00:30.775
In a normal courtroom drama, you'd be encouraged to

00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:33.775
root for the protagonist's client to win for some kind of justice

00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:36.705
to prevail, but director Otto Preminger does everything

00:00:36.705 --> 00:00:39.895
he can to remove justice from the equation

00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:42.125
The case itself is ambiguous

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.525
Clearly it was a revenge killing but revenge

00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:46.605
is no justification for murder

00:00:46.605 --> 00:00:47.415
Will the jury or the audience

00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:50.575
be sympathetic enough to go along with the claim of temporary

00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:51.215
insanity

00:00:51.215 --> 00:00:53.575
"... the unwritten law on my side"

00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:55.700
"The unwritten law is a myth, Lieutenanat"

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.560
Preminger challenges

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.800
that sympathy by making Manion generally unlikable, even cruel.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.685
The rape itself is never shown. The murder victim

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:06.745
and alleged rapist is never introduced. And Preminger

00:01:06.745 --> 00:01:09.595
adds doubt by making Laura flirty and clearly

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:12.745
unhappy in her marriage. What's more, it's made very obvious

00:01:12.745 --> 00:01:15.685
that Biegler is not interested in the case for its

00:01:15.685 --> 00:01:18.945
moral content, but for the legal challenge that it represents.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:21.885
That keeps us from identifying too strongly

00:01:21.885 --> 00:01:23.520
with the great Jamie Steward

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.920
"What you need is a legal peg

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.560
so that the jury can hang up there something on your behalf

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.020
What's your legal excuse?

00:01:31.025 --> 00:01:33.295
So what's the payoff of all this work, of designing a case that's

00:01:33.295 --> 00:01:36.265
messy. A defendant who's unsympathetic,

00:01:36.265 --> 00:01:38.975
a lawyer who's in it only for the challenge

00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:41.380
What happens when justice is removed

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:41.995
what are you left with?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.500
Well, if you're not telling me to cough out

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.860
what are you telling me to do?

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.260
"I'm not telling you to do anything. I just want you to understand

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.260
the letter of the law." That's it, the law, that's what makes "Anatomy of a Murder"

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.400
so special and unique. It's a movie

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.480
carefully designed to make the law visible.

00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:02.885
And what does the law look like? Well, it looks a lot like

00:02:02.885 --> 00:02:06.075
narrative. "What the defense is trying to do

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.895
is introduce some sensational material

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.600
for the purposes of obscuring the real issues."

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.180
"Your Honor, how can the jury accurately estimate the testimony

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.160
being given here unless they first know the reason

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.320
behind this whole trial?"

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.960
Each lawyer is trying to curve out

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.580
a story from the evidence. The prosecution

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.800
wants the jury to see the murder and the defense

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:28.540
wants the jury

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.460
to see the rape. The prosecution

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.580
wants the audience to see

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.820
the act, the defense wants the audience to see the motive.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.815
And in their efforts to place each fact in their desired context,

00:02:37.815 --> 00:02:40.875
the importance of words becomes more

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.380
and more and more apparent.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.440
"... the attorney for the people asked you if Miss Manion was tight

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.720
and you said that she was high.

00:02:50.315 --> 00:02:51.315
 

00:02:51.565 --> 00:02:54.585
Speaking as a bartender, what's the distinction between the two?"

00:02:54.585 --> 00:02:55.705
"I don't think I understand"

00:02:56.055 --> 00:02:59.155
"Well, when we say a person's tight

00:02:59.505 --> 00:03:00.505
we usually mean

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.200
that they're a little stupid with a drink

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.440
isn't that so?"

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.000
"I guess that's about it, yes"

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.340
"And if they're high, they're gay and enjoying themselves

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.580
"Yes"

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.080
"Then that's the word for 'Manion was happy'

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.580
well is there anything wrong with being happy?

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.180
"No."

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.860
The prosecution attempts to paint a picture of

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.760
Laura Manion as promiscuous but Biegler

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.480
drills down into the vocabulary to show

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.280
that suggestion from a different linguistic point of view

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.500
"What would you call a man with an isatiable penchant for women?"

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.200
Earlier in the trial Biegler made the same witness describe Barney Quill

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.135
by cycling through synonyms until he reached the word

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.140
"Wolf?"

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.300
"Sure, just slipped my mind.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.115
Once Mrs Manion's

00:03:40.115 --> 00:03:43.065
panties become relevant to the trial the judge

00:03:43.065 --> 00:03:45.505
asks the lawyers if they know an alternate term because

00:03:45.505 --> 00:03:48.200
of the juvenile affect that word is having on

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.300
the courtroom. [Laughters]

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.760
"There isn't anything comic about a pair of panties

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.100
which figure in the violent

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.620
death of one man

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.100
and the possible incarceration of another."

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.065
Watching from this unique perspective, this movie

00:04:04.065 --> 00:04:06.755
about a murder trial transforms into a

00:04:06.755 --> 00:04:09.755
a granular study about the construction of stories

00:04:09.755 --> 00:04:12.035
how language itself, how every word

00:04:12.035 --> 00:04:14.905
is loaded with history and when you choose a word

00:04:14.905 --> 00:04:17.815
you're choosing its history too. All the stories that go

00:04:17.815 --> 00:04:20.835
with it. Preminger deliberately guides us into

00:04:20.835 --> 00:04:23.805
this place, understanding I think that the courtroom

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:26.825
is a zone where all this mechanics of narrative

00:04:26.825 --> 00:04:29.725
play out and that if you could just remove that sense of justice

00:04:29.725 --> 00:04:32.745
those mechanics would become clear

00:04:32.745 --> 00:04:33.745
in a singular way.

00:04:35.065 --> 00:04:36.065
 

00:04:36.335 --> 00:04:38.065
 

00:04:38.685 --> 00:04:39.685
 

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:40.800
 

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.480
"Anatomy of a Murder" zooms in to show how story

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.400
is latent into language, intonation, and gesture

00:04:47.405 --> 00:04:50.215
and it zooms out to show how story

00:04:50.215 --> 00:04:53.285
is embedded in a fundamental part of

00:04:53.285 --> 00:04:56.295
the practice of law. It makes sense that the

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:59.255
thing which essentially locks in the victory for Biegler isn't some

00:04:59.255 --> 00:05:02.285
new piece of evidence but a moment in which his opponent's

00:05:02.285 --> 00:05:05.285
story is revealed to be unworkable.

